s://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Discovery and Early Career","body":"The band was discovered by the manager of The Allman Brothers Band, who heard them in Macon, Georgia. They subsequently signed with Capricorn, the same label as The Allman Brothers Band, and opened for them on tour. \"It was the manager of the Alman Brothers band that discovered the Marshall Tucker band when he heard them in make in Georgia.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 46 - The Chaos and Music of the Ozark Music Festival of 1974","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 46 - The Chaos and Music of the Ozark Music Festival of 1974","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Distinctive Southern Rock Sound","body":"Rock critic Richard Regal characterized their sound as featuring \"warm, honey laden guitar meshes\" and \"lyrical celebrations of simple virtues,\" which he felt resonated authentically because the band \"have always been back on their land,\" rather than adopting a \"back to the land\" escapist trend. The band's diverse instrumentation, including piano, horn, flute, sax, and fiddle alongside guitars, contributes to a sound that exemplifies the broad umbrella of Southern rock. \"the warm, honey laden guitar meshes, the lyrical celebrations of simple virtues which ring true because these guys aren't going back to the land as escapists from the urban willies.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 46 - The Chaos and Music of the Ozark Music Festival of 1974","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 46 - The Chaos and Music of the Ozark Music Festival of 1974","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Musical Lineage from Delaney & Bonnie","body":"The sound of The Marshall Tucker Band shows a clear lineage from Delaney & Bonnie, sharing stylistic similarities. \"You can hear it. I think you could draw a mostly straight line from Delaney and Bonnie to the Marshall Tucker band.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 59 - Bands and Booze on the Festival Express of 1970","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 59 - Bands and Booze on the Festival Express of 1970","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]},{"kind":"podcast","title":"Sound Similarities with The Band","body":"The Marshall Tucker Band also shared a similar sound with The Band. \"They also sound like the band.\"","year":null,"extra":null,"srcs":[{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 59 - Bands and Booze on the Festival Express of 1970","url":null},{"source":"For the Record - The 70s","episode":"Ep. 59 - Bands and Booze on the Festival Express of 1970","url":"https://www.ftr70.com/"}]}],"awards":[],"concerts":[{"kind":"concert","title":"Milwaukee Performing Arts Center (1974)","body":null,"year":1974,"extra":null,"srcs":[]},{"kind":"concert","title":"Ozark Music Festival (1974)","body":null,"year":1974,"extra":null,"srcs":[]}],"xref":[],"charts":[{"id":30371583724267,"type":"album","name":"The Marshall Tucker Band","list_id":109535037444116,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Country Rock","src":"musthear","rank":47,"year":1973,"note":null,"yt":"TOk4Tc3t3_c","scaruffi":null},{"id":30371583724267,"type":"album","name":"The Marshall Tucker Band","list_id":201960148920398,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Southern Rock","src":"musthear","rank":22,"year":1973,"note":null,"yt":"TOk4Tc3t3_c","scaruffi":null},{"id":89616781294044,"type":"album","name":"Where We All Belong","list_id":109535037444116,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Country Rock","src":"musthear","rank":52,"year":1974,"note":null,"yt":"q6ExGTJ5s5A","scaruffi":null},{"id":64411435886303,"type":"album","name":"A New Life","list_id":109535037444116,"list":"RYM Top — Rock — Country Rock","src":"musthear","rank":61,"year":1974,"note":null,"yt":"xo9HP3h8ASc","scaruffi":null},{"id":89616781